>> Only what R can handle. Keep in mind core has a an R process sharing
>> the total memory.
>> So if one sets rhipe_map_buff to say 100,000 - each core (== R
>> process) reads 100,000 subsets.
>> -  takes time creating this (there is a sweet spot, i think for 5K is
>> good, but well could be data specific)
>> - if 100,000 subsets is 1GB, and you have 16 cores each running R, the
>> 16 R's will end up trying to use 16GB! All hell breaks loose when
>> there is no free ram.
>>
>> But you might millions of subsets, Rhipe will always slowly work
>> through them. You can
>> (1) work through them one by one - which means calling an R function
>> once for every subset - very very slow
>> (2) buffer them (the 5k thing) and call the user R code to do its
>> stuff on this 5K, Rhipe then reads another 5K and calls the user R
>> function.
>>
>> (2) is a generalization of (1), where N=1.
>>
>> In Java and other languages, the user's code is called once for every
>> subset. Unfortunately, that would make incredibly inefficient for R
>> hence
>> the approach of calling the user's R code for batches of subsets.
>>
>> Saptarshi
